  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Suriname&#x2019;s border management systems</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Suriname faces increasing border management challenges driven by growing migration flows, irregular crossings, and risks linked to transnational crime, including migrant smuggling and human trafficking. Limited interoperability of existing border management systems restricts the ability of authorities to collect, analyze, and share migration data efficiently, affecting situational awareness, identity verification, and the protection of vulnerable populations. This project aims to strengthen sustainable, effective, and rights-based border governance in Suriname through the deployment and institutionalization of the Migration Information and Data Analysis System (MIDAS).The project will support technical and legal assessments for MIDAS deployment and establish the system at selected border crossing points and at the Directorate of National Security (DNS) headquarters. In parallel, it will strengthen institutional capacities by training ICT personnel and border officers, developing maintenance and sustainability mechanisms, and providing gender-sensitive standard operating procedures, user guides, and installation manuals. The formal handover of the system to the Government of Suriname will further support national ownership and long-term sustainability.As a result, the Directorate of National Security will be better equipped to operate, maintain, and manage an integrated border management information system in line with international standards, enhancing security, migration management, and data-driven decision-making.</narrative>
  </description>
